// E0453: a `match` subject that projects a droppable field out of an owned
// value moves it, exactly as the assignment spelling of the same projection
// does.
//
// The subject is evaluated into a drop-glued temporary before any arm runs, so
// the tuple and that temporary both own the payload and both free it.
// `mut v: Array<u8> = p.0;` was already rejected; the `match` spelling reached
// the arms unchecked, built cleanly, and died at runtime with
// STATUS_HEAP_CORRUPTION - precisely the silent failure this rule converts into
// a diagnostic.
//
// The copy into the temporary does NOT depend on an arm binding the payload:
// `Some(_)` double-freed the same way `Some(v)` did, so the rule does not look
// at the patterns.
//
// A TUPLE is the subject here on purpose. The partial-move model can allow (and
// track) a field moved out of a straight-line, non-`Drop` STRUCT local, and
// does so soundly for a match subject too - see
// lang/match_subject_owned_place.cryo, which pins that case at exactly one
// drop. A tuple is not a struct/class, so no per-field drop-skip is available
// and the conservative error stands.
![config(testing)]
![config(negative, E0453)]
namespace CryoTests::Negative::MatchSubjectFieldMove;

type struct Res {
    id: i64;

    static new(id: i64) -> Res { return Res { id: id }; }

    drop(mut &this) -> void { }
}

function main() -> int {
    mut p: (Res, i64) = (Res::new(7), 0);
    mut got: i64 = 0;
    match (p.0) {                   // the tuple still drops field 0 at scope exit
        _ => { got = 1; }
    }
    return got as int;
}
